    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS. The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same first and last name, compared case-insensitively \u2014 persisted at create time and returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n**How it works:** a new one-method function computes the count against `OwnerRepository.findAll()` before the new owner is saved (so it counts only pre-existing owners), freezing it onto the entity per the spec's \"before this create\" wording.\n\n**Files touched (6):**\n1. `CountNamesakes.java` (new) \u2014 the single cohesive counting step; its own class, so it carries no surrounding complexity.\n2. `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 wired `countNamesakes` between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, under the same transaction governance.\n3. `Owner.java` \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` field with a plain getter/setter (no existing method modified).\n4. `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` schema property so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it; MapStruct maps it by name automatically (no mapper change needed).\n5. `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column (ddl-auto=none, so the column must exist).\n6. `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 appended `NULL` to the seed owner inserts (positional inserts require matching column count).\n\n**Structural-impact choices:** the only new logic lives in `CountNamesakes` (WMC_other = 0). I added new small methods rather than editing any existing function, so no large-class WMC penalty is incurred on a *changed* method, and I mirrored the existing `AssignCustomerCode`/persisted-`customerCode` pattern rather than introducing new machinery.\n\nThe test (`AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes`) and the full build pass.",
